.\" Title: vrunner-list-vrunner
.\" Author: vrunner contributors
.\" Manual section: 1
.\" Process this file with
.\"   groff -man -Tascii vrunner-list-vrunner.1

.TH VRUNNER\-LIST\-VRUNNER 1 "2025" "vrunner" "User Commands"

.SH NAME
vrunner\-list\-vrunner \- list running vrunner instances in machine-readable format

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vrunner
\fBlist\-vrunner\fR

.SH DESCRIPTION
List all running vrunner instances in a compact, tab-separated (TSV) format suitable for parsing in scripts and automated tools. This is the machine-friendly alternative to \fBvrunner list\fR, which produces human-readable formatted output with ANSI colors.

Each line after the header represents one vrunner instance, with fields separated by tab characters. The header line is always emitted, making it easy to parse with tools like \fBawk\fR, \fBcut\fR, or any TSV-aware processor. Unreachable instances are silently skipped (no error row is emitted).

This subcommand queries the shared instance registry directly and does not connect to individual instances over HTTP. It only shows registry metadata, not the commands running inside each instance.

.SH OPTIONS

.TP
.BR \-\-target \fIPID\fR
Only list the vrunner instance with the given PID. This is a global option specified before the subcommand: \fBvrunner \-\-target 12345 list\-vrunner\fR.

.SH OUTPUT FORMAT

Tab-separated fields:

.TP
.B PID
Process ID of the vrunner instance.

.TP
.B PORT
TCP port the instance is listening on.

.TP
.B BIND
Bind address (e.g., \fB127.0.0.1\fR or \fB0.0.0.0\fR).

.TP
.B DAEMON
Whether the instance is running as a background daemon (\fByes\fR/\fBno\fR).

.TP
.B DISPLAY
Whether local VTTY display is enabled (\fByes\fR/\fBno\fR).

.TP
.B STARTUP_CMD
The initial command the instance was started with, or \fB(idle)\fR if no command was given.

.SH EXAMPLES

.PP
Basic usage:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner list\-vrunner\fR
PID     PORT    BIND            DAEMON   DISPLAY  STARTUP_CMD
12345   9090    127.0.0.1       no       no       (idle)
12346   8080    127.0.0.1       yes      no       htop
.fi
.RE

.PP
Parse with awk:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner list-vrunner | awk -F'\\t' 'NR>1 && $3=="0.0.0.0" {print $1}'\fR
12347
.fi
.RE

.PP
Count running instances:
.RS
.PP
.nf
\fB$ vrunner list-vrunner | tail -n +2 | wc -l\fR
2
.fi
.RE

.SH EXIT STATUS

.TP
.B 0
Successful listing (including empty results).

.TP
.B 1
Error (registry access failure, invalid \fB\-\-target\fR PID, etc.).

.SH SEE ALSO

.BR vrunner (1),
